WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.660
Back to e-graphs.

2
00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:03.491
Matrix accelerators

3
00:00:03.491 --> 00:00:05.415
such as a systolic array

4
00:00:05.415 --> 00:00:06.970
run matrix products.

5
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.082
A convolution is not one

6
00:00:09.082 --> 00:00:11.337
but it can be rewritten as one

7
00:00:11.337 --> 00:00:13.240
a trick called im-two-col.

8
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.450
Glenside, from MAPS 2021

9
00:00:15.524 --> 00:00:18.202
is a language in which such rewrites

10
00:00:18.276 --> 00:00:19.690
are pure and local

11
00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.070
so an e-graph can explore them.

12
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.399
In Glenside

13
00:00:23.399 --> 00:00:25.959
a tensor is viewed as an access pattern

14
00:00:26.021 --> 00:00:27.706
an outer shape that indexes

15
00:00:27.769 --> 00:00:29.142
a collection of items

16
00:00:29.142 --> 00:00:30.890
and the shape of each item.

17
00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.822
A tensor of two by three by four

18
00:00:33.822 --> 00:00:35.935
accessed at dimension one

19
00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.360
is two items of three by four.

20
00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:41.090
Windows makes one item per window

21
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.120
and a dot product multiplies

22
00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.870
pairs of items and sums.

23
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.468
The rewrites and the accelerator

24
00:00:47.532 --> 00:00:49.298
rule depend on these shapes

25
00:00:49.298 --> 00:00:52.010
so the e-graph keeps them for every class.

26
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:53.725
That is an analysis

27
00:00:53.793 --> 00:00:55.500
make builds a node's fact

28
00:00:55.568 --> 00:00:56.933
from its children's

29
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:59.800
and merge joins the facts of two classes.

30
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.770
Here, a two by two kernel slides over

31
00:01:02.841 --> 00:01:04.470
a three by three image.

32
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.926
The windows give four items

33
00:01:06.926 --> 00:01:09.860
each two by two, one per output pixel.

34
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.507
Flatten each window into a row

35
00:01:12.507 --> 00:01:14.840
and you get the im-two-col matrix.

36
00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:18.470
Multiply it by the kernel, one, zero, zero

37
00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.750
minus one, and every output is minus four.

38
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.062
In the e-graph

39
00:01:23.126 --> 00:01:25.115
rewrites add equal forms of the

40
00:01:25.179 --> 00:01:27.425
convolution until one has the shape

41
00:01:27.489 --> 00:01:28.900
the accelerator wants.

42
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.910
Without correct shapes

43
00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.930
no rewrite can fire safely.

44
00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.141
The task removes Glenside's analysis

45
00:01:36.141 --> 00:01:38.630
make and merge for every operator.

46
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.889
There are fourteen workloads

47
00:01:40.955 --> 00:01:42.600
twelve small convolutions

48
00:01:42.666 --> 00:01:44.180
and two concatenations.

49
00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.949
A workload is discovered if the extracted

50
00:01:47.008 --> 00:01:49.249
program uses the accelerator and gives

51
00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:50.370
the right numbers.

52
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.120
To pass, a run must discover wherever

53
00:01:53.185 --> 00:01:54.417
the reference does

54
00:01:54.482 --> 00:01:56.363
with no more rewrite time and

55
00:01:56.428 --> 00:01:57.920
no more e-graph growth.

56
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.100
Pause and predict.

57
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.310
The reference grows the e-graph by fifty-five

58
00:02:03.374 --> 00:02:05.090
thousand two hundred nodes.

59
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.600
How many do the best runs add?

60
00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.430
About eighteen.

61
00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.842
Twelve of sixteen models passed

62
00:02:15.842 --> 00:02:17.590
the most in this field.

63
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.969
Seven runs shared the best score

64
00:02:19.969 --> 00:02:22.612
ninety-one, against eighty-two point eight

65
00:02:22.674 --> 00:02:23.780
for the reference.

66
00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.311
They added about eighteen nodes in about

67
00:02:26.365 --> 00:02:27.946
one hundred ten microseconds

68
00:02:28.001 --> 00:02:29.964
where the reference took one hundred

69
00:02:30.018 --> 00:02:31.490
seventy-eight milliseconds.

70
00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.608
Claude Opus 5.5 rebuilt the dot product

71
00:02:34.679 --> 00:02:36.382
over flattened operands

72
00:02:36.453 --> 00:02:38.937
so the accelerator rule saw a plain

73
00:02:39.008 --> 00:02:40.640
matrix product at once.

74
00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.116
Muse Spark 1.3 grew the e-graph

75
00:02:43.184 --> 00:02:44.899
as much as the reference

76
00:02:44.899 --> 00:02:47.230
but was slower on four workloads

77
00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.190
so it failed.

78
00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.355
That ends the Programming Languages lecture

79
00:02:51.355 --> 00:02:52.530
with three lessons.

80
00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.189
One data structure, three tasks

81
00:02:55.189 --> 00:02:58.363
Egg maintains the e-graph, BABBLE mines it

82
00:02:58.363 --> 00:03:00.960
and Glenside attaches shapes to it.

83
00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.114
Every workload, every metric: in Egg

84
00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:06.767
thirteen runs beat the reference's

85
00:03:06.843 --> 00:03:08.510
score and none passed.

86
00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:12.060
And write less, not faster: in Glenside

87
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.940
a direct rewrite beat a big search.

88
00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:16.381
As an exercise

89
00:03:16.381 --> 00:03:19.654
compute im-two-col for a four by four image

90
00:03:19.654 --> 00:03:20.890
with stride two.

91
00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.000
Next time: Lecture 10, software engineering.
